---

<BaseLayout
  {title}
  {description}
  canonical="https://divorceu.ae/en/name-change-after-divorce-uae/"
  {hreflang}
  lang="en"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pt-4">
    <Breadcrumb items={breadcrumbs} />
  </div>

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="hero-section">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <p class="hero-tag">Post-Divorce Admin Guide, UAE 2025</p>
      <h1>Name Change After Divorce UAE: Complete Step-by-Step Guide</h1>
      <p class="hero-desc">
        There is no automatic name change when you divorce in UAE. If you want to revert to your maiden name, or simply update your marital status across 12 different institutions, you need to work through each one in the right sequence. This guide gives you the exact steps, fees, timelines, and what each institution actually requires.
      </p>
      <p class="hero-author">By <strong>Aleksi Suon</strong>, UAE Expat &amp; Legal Consultant &middot; Updated August 2026</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="page-layout">
    <main class="main-content">

      <!-- Key facts box -->
      <div class="answer-box">
        <h2>Before you start: four things to know</h2>
        <ul class="key-facts">
          <li><strong>Reverting to your maiden name is optional.</strong> There is no UAE law requiring it. You can keep your married name indefinitely.</li>
          <li><strong>Start with the Emirates ID.</strong> Almost every other institution requires an updated Emirates ID before they will process your request.</li>
          <li><strong>Get multiple certified copies of your divorce judgment.</strong> Banks, the RTA, and your landlord all want original copies, not photocopies.</li>
          <li><strong>Allow 2-3 months to complete everything.</strong> The Emirates ID itself is fast (3-7 days). Passport renewal through your home country embassy is the longest step.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 1: The legal framework -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>The Legal Position: No Automatic Change</h2>
        <p>
          UAE law does not operate an automatic name change system on divorce. When a Personal Status Court issues a divorce judgment, it records the termination of the marriage, it does not send notifications to the ICP, the RTA, your bank, or your landlord. Each institution you deal with in UAE holds its own record of your name and status, and each must be updated separately.
        </p>
        <p>
          The core document for all subsequent updates is the <strong>certified divorce judgment</strong> from the Personal Status Court. Without this, no UAE authority will process a marital status or name change. Once you have this document, attested and in some cases translated into Arabic, it unlocks every subsequent step.
        </p>
        <p>
          Whether you choose to revert to your maiden name or keep your married name after divorce is entirely your decision. There is no legal advantage or disadvantage to either choice in UAE law. Many women who share a surname with their children choose to keep the married name to avoid explaining discrepancies at schools, hospitals, and official appointments.
        </p>

        <div class="info-box info-box--info">
          <h3>Non-UAE divorces: attestation is essential</h3>
          <p>If your divorce was granted by a court outside the UAE, in the UK, India, the Philippines, or elsewhere, the foreign certificate must be attested before UAE institutions will accept it. The attestation chain is: ministry in the country of issue, then UAE embassy in that country, then MOFAIC in UAE. Only then is it accepted for Emirates ID and other document updates. See our <a href="/en/divorce-certificate-attestation-uae/">divorce certificate attestation guide</a> for the full procedure.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: Full checklist -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>The Complete Update Sequence: All 12 Steps</h2>
        <p>
          Work through these in order. Steps 1-3 (divorce judgment, attestation, Emirates ID) are the foundation, all other institutions require your updated Emirates ID before processing the change.
        </p>

        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>#</th>
                <th>Institution</th>
                <th>What to update</th>
                <th>Fee</th>
                <th>Timeline</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {updateChecklist.map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? '' : 'row-alt'}>
                  <td class="td-step">{row.step}</td>
                  <td class="td-bold">{row.institution}</td>
                  <td class="td-action">{row.action}</td>
                  <td class="td-fee">{row.fee}</td>
                  <td class="td-time">{row.timeline}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="note-text">Fees are indicative based on 2025 official schedules. Individual banks may have their own processes. Check with each institution before attending.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: Step by step detail -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Key Steps in Detail</h2>

        <h3>Step 1-2: Divorce Judgment and MOFAIC Attestation</h3>
        <p>
          Request a minimum of six certified copies of your divorce judgment from the Personal Status Court at the time of collection. Certified copies from the court are accepted by most UAE institutions. For foreign use (your home country embassy), you will additionally need MOFAIC attestation, the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs' stamp that authenticates the court's signature.
        </p>
        <p>
          MOFAIC attestation costs AED 150 per document and is processed in 1-3 business days at MOFAIC service centres or through their online portal. If the document is in Arabic only, you may need a certified English translation for your home country embassy, UAE-certified translators typically charge AED 50-150 per page.
        </p>

        <h3>Step 3: Emirates ID Update (ICP)</h3>
        <p>
          The ICP (Federal Authority for Identity, Citizenship, Customs and Ports Security) is the single most important update. Your Emirates ID reflects your full name, marital status, and visa category. Once updated, it serves as the proof of identity and divorce status for almost all subsequent steps.
        </p>
        <p>
          You can apply through an ICP-authorised typing centre (found in every mall and commercial area in UAE) or through the ICP smart app. You will need: the original or certified copy of the divorce judgment, your current Emirates ID card, a recent biometric passport photo, and the application fee of AED 100-200 depending on the card validity requested. Processing takes 3-7 working days after submission. You are notified by SMS when the new card is ready for collection.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you are reverting to your maiden name, the name on your Emirates ID will change to match your passport name. If you are keeping your married name but updating marital status only, the name stays the same but the status field changes from "Married" to "Divorced."
        </p>

        <h3>Step 4: Home Country Passport Renewal</h3>
        <p>
          Name changes on passports are handled by your home country's embassy or consulate in UAE, not by UAE authorities. Each country has its own procedure. Common requirements across most embassies include: the original passport, the attested and translated divorce certificate, a completed application form, passport photos, and the relevant consular fee.
        </p>
        <p>
          British nationals apply through the HM Passport Office online with the divorce certificate as evidence for reverting to a former name. Indian nationals apply through the Indian embassy in Abu Dhabi or consulate in Dubai for an ECR or non-ECR passport. Philippine nationals apply through the Philippine Overseas Labour Office or DFA accredited centres. Processing times vary significantly, European embassies are typically faster (4-8 weeks) than South Asian embassies (6-12 weeks or more).
        </p>
        <p>
          Once your new passport is issued, bring it to ICP for an update to your Emirates ID if the document number changes, visa stamps are linked to your passport number.
        </p>

        <h3>Step 6: Driving Licence (RTA Dubai / Transport Authority)</h3>
        <p>
          The Roads and Transport Authority in Dubai updates driving licences efficiently. Bring your updated Emirates ID (reflecting the new name if you have changed it), the divorce certificate, and AED 100. The update is typically processed in a single visit at any RTA customer service centre. In Abu Dhabi, apply to Abu Dhabi Police Traffic Department. In Sharjah, to the Sharjah Roads and Transport Authority. Each emirate has its own transport authority but the process is similar.
        </p>

        <h3>Step 7: Bank Accounts</h3>
        <p>
          Each bank has its own internal procedure for processing a name change after divorce. There is no cross-bank notification system. You must visit each bank individually or submit through their mobile banking documentation upload (where available).
        </p>
        <p>
          Typical requirements: your updated Emirates ID, the original attested divorce certificate or certified copy, and a completed bank form for customer detail amendments. Some banks require you to visit the branch where the account was opened. Processing takes 1-5 business days. If you have accounts at multiple banks, Emirates NBD, FAB, ADCB, Mashreq, and others each have separate processes, budget one business day per bank.
        </p>
        <p>
          Do not forget joint accounts. Joint accounts opened during the marriage need to be reviewed: you may want to convert to a sole account, close the joint account, or formally remove your ex-spouse's access. This requires the bank's own process and in some cases both parties' presence or written instructions.
        </p>

        <h3>Step 8: Ejari (Tenancy Contract) in Dubai</h3>
        <p>
          If you have a tenancy registered under Ejari (Dubai's mandatory tenancy registration system), the tenant's name and Emirates ID on the contract must match your current ID. To update Ejari: visit a Dubai Land Department service centre or an Ejari-authorised typing centre with your updated Emirates ID, divorce certificate, and the existing Ejari registration certificate. Fee is approximately AED 100-200. Processing takes 1-3 business days.
        </p>
        <p>
          In Abu Dhabi, the equivalent system is Tawtheeq (managed by Abu Dhabi Municipality). The process is similar. If the lease was in your ex-spouse's name only, you will need the landlord to issue a new lease agreement in your name or an assignment of the existing lease with the landlord's consent.
        </p>

        <h3>Step 10: Employer and Employment Visa</h3>
        <p>
          Notify your HR department of your change in marital status and name if applicable. Your employer may need to update their records and may need to amend your employment contract if your name changes. Depending on your visa category, your name change may require a visa amendment. Employment visas (sponsored by your employer) that include your name typically need the employer to initiate a visa amendment through the Ministry of Human Resources and Emiratisation (MOHRE). Your employer's PRO (public relations officer) will handle this.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is also the time to check your sponsorship status if you were previously on a spouse visa. See our <a href="/en/visa-after-divorce-uae/">visa after divorce UAE guide</a> for the options available.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4: Children's names -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Changing Children's Names After Divorce</h2>
        <p>
          Children's surnames are a separate and more complex matter. UAE law does not permit one parent to change a child's registered name without the other parent's consent or a court order. Courts apply a high threshold for ordering a name change against a parent's wishes, as stability of name is considered to be in the child's interests.
        </p>
        <p>
          If both parents agree to change the child's surname, the process involves: a joint application to the Personal Status Court or relevant registry, supported by both parents' consent, the divorce judgment, and the child's birth certificate. The court or registry will process the change and issue updated documents.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you want to hyphenate the child's surname or add your maiden name as an additional surname, this also requires the guardian's consent (see our guide on <a href="/en/guardianship-vs-custody-uae/">guardianship vs custody UAE</a>). For practical purposes, many custodial mothers find it simpler to keep their own married name matching the children's surname, avoiding this issue entirely.
        </p>

        <div class="info-box info-box--warning">
          <h3>Inconsistent documents: a frequent practical problem</h3>
          <p>Many divorced women in UAE find themselves in a period where their Emirates ID has been updated but their passport, driving licence, and bank account still show the married name. This is normal during the transition period. For day-to-day transactions, the Emirates ID is the primary document in UAE and institutions will typically accept it as the current record. Keep a certified copy of the divorce judgment with you during this transition period to explain any name discrepancies if challenged.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section: updating a child's records -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Updating a Child's Records After Divorce</h2>
        <p>
          A UAE divorce changes none of your child's records automatically, and in most cases the child's name stays exactly as it was registered. What usually needs attention is not the child's name but the details of who holds custody, who pays, and who the school or hospital should contact. These updates are separate from any surname change and do not require the other parent's consent.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>School records.</strong> Give the school your updated Emirates ID, a copy of the divorce judgment, and the custody arrangement so the emergency contact, fee payer, and authorised pick-up list are correct. This is where inconsistent records cause the most day-to-day friction. Our <a href="/en/child-custody-uae/">child custody UAE guide</a> explains how custody and access are set out in the judgment.</li>
          <li><strong>Residence visa and Emirates ID.</strong> If the child was sponsored on the father's visa and sponsorship shifts to the mother, the child's visa file is updated through GDRFA or ICA. The child's own name does not change through this, only the sponsor on record.</li>
          <li><strong>Health insurance.</strong> Confirm which parent's policy now covers the child and update the contact and beneficiary details so claims are not rejected during the transition.</li>
          <li><strong>Passport.</strong> A child's passport is reissued only if the child's own name changes, which needs both parents' consent as covered above. A divorce on its own does not require a new passport for the child.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Section: passport in husband's name -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Expats Whose Passport Already Carries the Husband's Name</h2>
        <p>
          For several nationalities a married woman's passport is issued in, or amended to add, her husband's family name. This is common for some Gulf and Levantine conventions, parts of Africa, and women of any nationality who formally adopted the married surname on marriage. Reverting is a home-country procedure, not a UAE one, and the UAE has no power to change what your passport says. Only your embassy or national authority can do that.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first question is whether reversion is even available, and whether it happens automatically on divorce or needs a separate application. Both answers depend entirely on your nationality's rules. Some countries revert a woman to her maiden name automatically once the divorce is registered in their civil records. Others treat the married name as your legal name until you actively apply to change it, sometimes requiring a deed poll or an equivalent formal step. A minority do not permit reversion at all while the divorce remains unregistered at home.
        </p>
        <p>
          Because of this, the order of operations for these expats is different from the standard sequence. You often need to register or recognise the divorce in your home country's civil registry first, then apply for the amended passport, and only then bring the new passport to ICP to align your Emirates ID and residence visa. Check with your embassy before starting any UAE-side update, because your Emirates ID name should match your passport, not lead it.
        </p>
        <div class="info-box info-box--warning">
          <h3>Do not let the old passport lapse before the visa is transferred</h3>
          <p>Until your new passport is issued, your UAE residence visa stays linked to the old passport number. Do not cancel or allow the old passport to expire before the visa is moved to the new document, or you risk a gap in residence status. Once the new passport is in hand, take it to ICP so the Emirates ID and visa both point to it. For the paperwork you will need at each stage, see our guides on <a href="/en/documents-for-divorce-uae/">documents for divorce in UAE</a> and the wider <a href="/en/divorce-in-uae/">divorce in UAE process</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5: Non-UAE nationals -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Non-UAE Nationals: Updating Home Country Records</h2>
        <p>
          Updating your UAE documents is only half the process for expatriates. Your home country records, national identity card, voter registration, social security or equivalent, and tax records, also need to reflect your new status if you reverted to your maiden name.
        </p>

        <h3>European nationals</h3>
        <p>
          British nationals: update the DVLA driving licence (UK), inform HMRC of the name change, update your UK bank accounts if you maintain them, and update the UK electoral roll if registered. French, German, and other EU nationals should contact their local municipality office (commune, Standesamt, commune administrative) via their embassy in UAE for guidance on updating national civil registry records from abroad.
        </p>

        <h3>South Asian nationals</h3>
        <p>
          Indian nationals should update their Aadhaar card, PAN card, and voter ID through the relevant Indian authorities. This can be initiated through the Indian embassy in UAE with the attested UAE divorce certificate as supporting documentation. Pakistani nationals should contact NADRA through the Pakistani consulate. Philippine nationals should contact the PSA (Philippine Statistics Authority) through the Philippine Overseas Labour Office in Dubai.
        </p>

        <h3>Australian and New Zealand nationals</h3>
        <p>
          Australian nationals can apply for a new Australian passport in their reverted name through the Australian embassy, using the UAE divorce certificate as evidence. Centrelink and Australian Tax Office records can be updated by contacting those agencies directly with the divorce documentation.
        </p>
        <p>
          The key point is that your UAE divorce certificate, once properly attested and translated, is accepted evidence by almost all national authorities worldwide. The question is procedural: which authority in your home country processes the update, and what additional documentation they require alongside the divorce certificate.
        </p>
      </section>

